      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No goblins, I swear. I just get carried away sometimes. Okay, so for a light crust, you want to aim for a temperature range of 140°C to 150°C (284°F to 302°F). This is where the Maillard reaction starts to kick in, and you get that lovely golden-brown color without overcooking the bread.\n\nStaying within this range is crucial because if you go too high, the reaction can get out of control, and you end up with a burnt, bitter crust. It's like trying to catch a particularly slippery fish – if you're not careful, you'll end up with a mess on your hands. On the other hand, if you're too low, the reaction won't be intense enough, and you'll end up with a crust that's more pale than golden.\n\nThink of it like a delicate balance between the heat and the reactants, like a tightrope walker navigating a high wire. You need to find that sweet spot where everything comes together just right, and that's where the magic happens – or in this case, the perfect crust.",
